    "q-49929": {
      "dom_id": "step-q49929",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{1}4({z}^{2}+1){e}^{2z}\\,\\text{d}z.$",
      "worked_solution": "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={z}^{2}+1\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}z}=2z, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}z}=4{e}^{2z}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}z}\\,\\text{d}z=4\\int {e}^{2z}\\,\\text{d}z=2{e}^{2z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4({z}^{2}+1){e}^{2z}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =({z}^{2}+1)(2{e}^{2z})-\\int (2{e}^{2z})(2z)\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2({z}^{2}+1){e}^{2z}-4\\int z{e}^{2z}\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\int z{e}^{2z}\\text{d}z.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=z\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}z}=1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}z}={e}^{2z}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}z}\\,\\text{d}z=\\int {e}^{2z}\\,\\text{d}z=\\frac{{e}^{2z}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int z{e}^{2z}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =(z)(\\frac{{e}^{2z}}{2})-\\int (\\frac{{e}^{2z}}{2})(1)\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\frac{z{e}^{2z}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{2z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\frac{z{e}^{2z}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{2z}}{4}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4({z}^{2}+1){e}^{2z}\\,\\text{d}z & =2({z}^{2}+1){e}^{2z}-4\\int z{e}^{2z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2({z}^{2}+1){e}^{2z}-4(\\frac{z{e}^{2z}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{2z}}{4}+D) \\\\  & =2({z}^{2}+1){e}^{2z}-2z{e}^{2z}+{e}^{2z}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-4D.$ \n\nFinally, then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4({z}^{2}+1){e}^{2z}\\,\\text{d}z & =(2({z}^{2}+1){e}^{2z}-2z{e}^{2z}+{e}^{2z}){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(2({1}^{2}+1){(e)}^{2}-2(1)^{2}{e}^{2}+{e}^{2})-(2(0+1){e}^{0}-2\\cdot 0\\cdot {e}^{0}+{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{2}-3\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
