    "q-12701": {
      "dom_id": "step-q12701",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Calculate the integral $\\int ({x}^{2}+3x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$",
      "worked_solution": "We need to use integration by parts. \n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={x}^{2}+3x\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x+3 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({x}^{2}+3x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+3x)(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))(2x+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+3x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+2\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\text{d}x+3\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+3x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+2\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\text{d}x+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we use integration by parts to evaluate $\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))(1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({x}^{2}+3x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-({x}^{2}+3x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+2(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+D)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+3x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+C+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) \\\\  & =(-{x}^{2}-3x+2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+(2x+3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=2D.$",
