    "q-114874": {
      "dom_id": "step-q114874",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$",
      "worked_solution": "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={x}^{2}+1\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+1)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we use integration by parts to evaluate $\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(1)dx \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2(-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+D) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-2D.$ Finally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={[({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =({\\pi }^{2}+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -(({0}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+2\\cdot 0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡0-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =({\\pi }^{2}+1)\\cdot 0+2\\pi (-1)-2\\cdot 0-0 \\\\  & =-2\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
