    "q-114887": {
      "dom_id": "step-q114887",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1}^{e}({log}_{2}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
      "worked_solution": "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=({log}_{2}⁡x)^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2{log}_{2}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\,\\text{d}x=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({log}_{2}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={({log}_{2}⁡x)}^{2}(x)-\\int (x)(\\frac{2{log}_{2}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{({log}_{2}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\int {log}_{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate  $\\int {log}_{2}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={log}_{2}⁡x\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\,\\text{d}x=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {log}_{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({log}_{2}⁡x)(x)-\\int (x)(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{log}_{2}⁡x-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{log}_{2}⁡x-\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({log}_{2}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =x{({log}_{2}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(x{log}_{2}⁡x-\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+D) \\\\  & =x{({log}_{2}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{2x{log}_{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+\\frac{2x}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{x{ln}^{2}⁡x}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}-\\frac{2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}+\\frac{2x}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}({ln}^{2}⁡x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+2)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-\\frac{2D}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.$\n\nNote that in the above calculation we used the change of base formula ${log}_{2}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.$\n\nFinally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}({log}_{2}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{x}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}({ln}^{2}⁡x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+2)]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}({ln}^{2}⁡e-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+2)-\\frac{1}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}({ln}^{2}⁡1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}-\\frac{2}{{ln}^{2}⁡2} \\\\  & =\\frac{e-2}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
