    },
    "q-114900": {
      "dom_id": "step-q114900",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1}^{e}4x({log}_{3}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
      "worked_solution": "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=({log}_{3}⁡x)^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2{log}_{3}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=4x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int 4x\\,\\text{d}x=2{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4x({log}_{3}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={({log}_{3}⁡x)}^{2}(2{x}^{2})-\\int (2{x}^{2})(\\frac{2{log}_{3}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}({log}_{3}⁡x)^{2}-\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\int x{log}_{3}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\int x{log}_{3}⁡(x)\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={log}_{3}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int x\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{log}_{3}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({log}_{3}⁡x)(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})-\\int (\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}{log}_{3}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}{log}_{3}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4x({log}_{3}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{x}^{2}({log}_{3}⁡x)^{2}-\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(\\frac{{x}^{2}{log}_{3}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+D) \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}({log}_{3}⁡x)^{2}-\\frac{2{x}^{2}{log}_{3}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}{ln}^{2}⁡x}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}-\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(2{ln}^{2}⁡x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-\\frac{4D}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}.$ \n\nNote that in the above calculation we used the change of base formula ${log}_{3}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}.$\n\nFinally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}4x({log}_{3}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(2{ln}^{2}⁡x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(2{ln}^{2}⁡e-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+1)-\\frac{{1}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(2{ln}^{2}⁡1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(2-2+1)-\\frac{1}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(0-0+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
