    "q-352040": {
      "dom_id": "step-q352040",
      "name": "Question 14",
      "problem": "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1/3}^{e/3}3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{2} & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=3 & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int 3\\,\\text{d}x=3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{2}(3x)-\\int (3x)(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{2}-\\int 6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\int 6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x) & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=6 & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int 6\\,\\text{d}x=6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)(6x)-\\int (6x)(\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)-\\int 6\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)-6x+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =3x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{2}-\\int 6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{2}-(6x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)-6x+D) \\\\  & =3x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{2}-6x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)+6x-D \\\\  & =3x((\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)+2)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-D.$\n\nFinally, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/3}^{e/3}3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{2}\\text{d}x & ={[3x((\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)+2)]}_{1/3}^{e/3} \\\\  & =[3(\\frac{e}{3})({(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{e}{3}))}^{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{e}{3})+2)]-[3(\\frac{1}{3})({(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}))}^{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{1}{3})+2)] \\\\  & =[e((\\operatorname{ln}⁡e)^{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+2)]-[1((\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)^{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+2)] \\\\  & =[e(1-2+2)]-[1(0-0+2)] \\\\  & =e-2\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
